---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<Base
  title="The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards"
  description="Two players claim cells of an n by n grid; the first to own four corners of a square wins. The first player wins exactly when n is at least 5, by a machine-checked strategy. Play against it."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</h1>
    <p class="byline">Sean Reid · August 2026</p>
  </header>

  <p class="lead">
    Two players take turns claiming cells of an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid. The first to hold all four corners
    of an axis-aligned square wins; a full board with no square is a draw. Erickson
    asked who wins. One explicit strategy wins for the first player on every board
    from 6 × 6 up, and a machine-checked proof covers all sizes at once: the first
    player wins exactly when <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />.
  </p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <span class="label">Theorem.</span> The first player has a winning strategy in
    the square achievement game on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board if and only if
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />. For <span set:html={k("n \\le 4")} /> the game
    is a draw under optimal play.
  </div>

  <p>
    Draws at 3 × 3 and 4 × 4 and a first-player win at 5 × 5 were known from
    computer search; from 15 × 15 up, strategy stealing gave the first player a
    win but no strategy. Sizes 6 through 14 resisted until August 2026, when
    first-player wins there were announced by computer search, one size at a
    time. The proof here is different in kind: one explicit strategy, two
    reflexes and a lookup table whose plans stay inside a 6 × 6 corner of the
    board, wins every board from 6 × 6 up, with a separate table for 5 × 5, and
    the whole argument, every board size at once, is checked in Lean 4.
  </p>

  <figure class="game">
    <sag-board n="5"></sag-board>
    <figcaption>
      Play against the strategy. The bot moves first and replays the proof's
      tables move for move; you reply by clicking a cell, or with the arrow keys
      and Enter. Change n to explore; below 5 × 5 neither side can force a win.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <nav class="routes" aria-label="where to go next">
    <a class="route" href={`${base}downloads/threshold.pdf`}>
      <span class="route-title">Read the paper</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        The definitions, the strategy, the verification runs, and the simulation
        argument that carries one checked board to all larger ones. PDF, built
        from this repository.
      </span>
    </a>
    <a class="route" href={`${base}threshold/proof/`}>
      <span class="route-title">See how the proof works</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        An illustrated guide: forcing plans, why one table serves every board,
        what a computer search has to be trusted with, and what the Lean
        development checks.
      </span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="route"
      href={`${base}downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz`}
    >
      <span class="route-title">Check it yourself</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        The Lean 4 development with the tables as data. A successful
        <code>lake build</code> is the proof; the README shows how to print the theorem
        and its axioms.
      </span>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <h2>What you have to trust</h2>
  <p>
    The headline statement in the development reads as follows, with
    <code>P1CanWin</code> the first player forcing a win and the two draw predicates
    each player securing at least a draw.
  </p>
  <pre
    class="lean"><code>theorem outcome (n : ℕ) :
    (P1CanWin n ∅ ∅ ↔ 5 ≤ n) ∧
      ((P1CanDraw n ∅ ∅ ∧ P2CanDraw n ∅ ∅) ↔ n ≤ 4)</code></pre>
  <p>
    The definitions behind it, cells, squares, and the winning predicate as a
    strategy tree, are a few dozen lines and are the only part a skeptic has to
    read. Beyond them the trust is Lean's kernel, mathlib, and
    <code>native_decide</code>, which lets the Lean compiler evaluate the
    exhaustive checks instead of the kernel. The tables were found by a Python
    search, but nothing about that search enters the proof: a wrong table fails
    the check.
  </p>
</Base>
